## 6. Input Features

The prediction API accepts the following features:

| Feature                   | Description                          |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `blood_group`             | Blood group such as O+, A+, B+, etc. |
| `month`                   | Month of the year, 1–12              |
| `day_of_week`             | Day of the week, 0–6                 |
| `season`                  | Seasonal category                    |
| `current_blood_stock`     | Current available blood units        |
| `previous_demand`         | Previous demand                      |
| `average_daily_usage`     | Average daily blood usage            |
| `number_of_donations`     | Number of donations                  |
| `incoming_blood_units`    | Expected incoming blood units        |
| `hospital_requests`       | Number of hospital requests          |
| `emergency_cases`         | Number of emergency cases            |
| `previous_week_demand`    | Demand during the previous week      |
| `previous_month_demand`   | Demand during the previous month     |
| `days_of_stock_remaining` | Estimated remaining stock duration   |

---

## 7. Model Performance

The current models were trained and evaluated using the synthetic development dataset.

### Demand Prediction

| Metric |  Score |
| ------ | -----: |
| MAE    | 4.1292 |
| RMSE   | 5.2064 |
| R²     | 0.8662 |

### Shortage Risk Prediction

| Metric    |  Score |
| --------- | -----: |
| Accuracy  | 0.8925 |
| Precision | 0.8928 |
| Recall    | 0.8925 |
| F1 Score  | 0.8926 |

These results are based on synthetic data and are intended for project development and demonstration.

---

## 8. Dataset

The development dataset contains 15,000 synthetic records covering the period from 2023 to 2025.

The dataset includes information related to:

* Blood group
* Blood stock
* Previous demand
* Average daily usage
* Donations
* Incoming blood units
* Hospital requests
* Emergency cases
* Previous week/month demand
* Seasonality
* Days of stock remaining
* Future demand
* Shortage risk

The dataset is generated using:

```text
src/data/generate_dataset.py
```

Because the dataset is synthetic, this project should not be used for real-world medical or operational decision-making without validation using appropriate real-world data.

---

## 9. Data Preprocessing

The preprocessing pipeline performs:

* Dataset loading
* Feature selection
* Categorical feature encoding
* Numerical feature processing
* Time-based train/test splitting

Categorical features include:

* Blood group
* Season

The data is split chronologically:

* Training data: before 2025
* Testing data: 2025

This avoids randomly mixing future records into the training data.

---

## 10. XGBoost Models

The project uses XGBoost for both machine-learning tasks.

### Demand Model

```text
XGBRegressor
```

The model predicts future blood demand as a numerical value.

### Shortage Model

```text
XGBClassifier
```

The model predicts:

```text
Low
Medium
High
```

A saved label encoder converts the model's numerical class representation back into the corresponding shortage-risk category.

---

## 11. Running the Backend

### Step 1: Activate the virtual environment

Windows PowerShell:

```powershell
.venv\Scripts\activate
```

### Step 2: Start FastAPI

From the project root:

```powershell
uvicorn api.main:app --reload
```

The API will run at:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8000
```

---

## 12. API Documentation

FastAPI automatically provides interactive Swagger documentation.

Open:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs
```

The API can be tested directly from the Swagger interface.

---

## 13. API Endpoints

### Health Check

```text
GET /health
```

Example response:

```json
{
  "status": "healthy",
  "service": "Blood Bank Demand & Shortage Prediction API"
}
```

### Prediction

```text
POST /predict
```

Example request:

```json
{
  "blood_group": "O+",
  "month": 10,
  "day_of_week": 4,
  "season": "Autumn",
  "current_blood_stock": 60,
  "previous_demand": 78,
  "average_daily_usage": 11.2,
  "number_of_donations": 8,
  "incoming_blood_units": 10,
  "hospital_requests": 18,
  "emergency_cases": 3,
  "previous_week_demand": 80,
  "previous_month_demand": 76,
  "days_of_stock_remaining": 5.4
}
```

Example response:

```json
{
  "predicted_demand": 80.34,
  "shortage_risk": "Medium"
}
```

---

## 14. React Frontend Integration

The React frontend can send a POST request to:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict
```

Example JavaScript request:

```javascript
const response = await fetch("http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    blood_group: "O+",
    month: 10,
    day_of_week: 4,
    season: "Autumn",
    current_blood_stock: 60,
    previous_demand: 78,
    average_daily_usage: 11.2,
    number_of_donations: 8,
    incoming_blood_units: 10,
    hospital_requests: 18,
    emergency_cases: 3,
    previous_week_demand: 80,
    previous_month_demand: 76,
    days_of_stock_remaining: 5.4
  })
});

const data = await response.json();

console.log(data.predicted_demand);
console.log(data.shortage_risk);
```

The backend currently allows requests from React development servers running on:

```text
http://localhost:5173
http://localhost:3000
```

